# Tracer: one target `LitexObject` may carry several independent memberships.
#
# Before this ABI rewrite, To-Lean bound `a` to the native Lean type `Complex`
# because the source parameter was `a C`. After the proof derived `a $in R`,
# the old emitter still rejected `f(a)` as a Complex-to-Real downcast.
#
# Current contract: `a`, `f`, every set, and every function-space object have
# Lean type `LitexObject`. The source binders retain these independent facts:
#
#   Litex.In a Litex.C
#   Litex.In f (Litex.FnSet ...)
#   Litex.In a Litex.R
#
# Equality transport proves the third fact from `a = 1` and `1 $in R`. The
# exact retained proof of `a $in R` is then used to build `Applicable f [a]`;
# no Lean retyping, widening, downcast, or inferred membership is involved.
forall a C, f fn(x R) R:
    a = 1
    =>:
        1 $in R
        a $in R
        f(a) = f(a)

# Rejected boundary (kept as a comment so this positive tracer verifies):
#
# forall a C, f fn(x R) R:
#     f(a) = f(a)
#
# The second program has no proof that `a $in R`. To-Lean must not manufacture
# an application certificate merely because both objects have target type
# `LitexObject`.
#
# Source gate:
# target/release/litex -compact -isolated -runner -f examples/05_compiler_interop/compile_to_lean_litex_object_abi.lit
# Focused compiler gates:
# cargo test --release universal_object_tracer_uses_membership_facts_without_retyping -- --nocapture
# cargo test --release universal_object_application_does_not_invent_domain_membership -- --nocapture
# Real Lean gate:
# LITEX_LEAN_PROJECT=/absolute/path/to/mathlib LITEX_LAKE=/absolute/path/to/lake cargo test --release universal_object_tracer_compiles_with_mathlib -- --ignored --nocapture
